This version focuses on developing the redactable map, which is in fact JPanel (paintPanel in Window.java)
on which user will paint his tiles and units by mouseClicks.

Because map consists of different colors i need a way repaint a map which user is redacting after the window was
invalidated by size change or hide/show.
The solution to this problem is buffered image in which all maps paint actions will be duplicated and later used
in case of window invalidation to copy whole image on JPanel at once.

Here arises another issue there is a need
to separate paint instructions used for different cases and at the same tile it'll be better for the code
extendability to avoid usage of conditional statements. The idea is to use state pattern and for that purpose
interface PaintInstruction and class InstructedPaintPanel were created. paintInstruction is in fact "instructions"
of what exactly should be painted on JPanel. InstructedPaintPanel uses instruction object in its paintComponent
method to realise code inside PaintInstruction.

image buffer and instructed panel will be used together, because every paint action on the panel will be
duplicated on buffer, therefore there is a need to contain them in the class there painting they both will be
painted. There is two approaches for that inheritance and aggregation. Inheritance may give us more extendability
in the way that we can use this class in the place of instructed panel, however here arises the problem with
default paint instruction, which is supposed to be a buffer image coping, seeing that must be a way to set
default paint instruction to instructed panel. In case with inheritance with can create a method in new class
but that will destroy the purpose of us using inheritance, because we won't be able to use this new class as
instructed panel. We can set default instruction in paintComponent method, but this way it will be called every
time panel paints something or default paint set instruction can be place inside other paintInstruction classes,
this however will make them more complex and less reusable. For purpose of setting default instruction it is more
beneficial to use aggregation which still allows us to use different types of instructed panel inside it.

Tiles are painted by processing mouse click events in which we need coordinate of mouse click to determine in
which grid cell tile is need to be painted. (In this version tiles are painted outside grid).

Instructions
    GridInstruction - paints grid of provided of provided size and color.
    PaintImage - paints stores image.
    PaintTile - paints tile in provided position, which is set method setPosition().
Coordinate - describes tile position in grid by row and column numbers.
The problem of setting default instruction still stands. Now BufferedPaintPanelV2 calls
SwingUtilities.invokeLater() for setting default instruction for every time instruction changes.

I don't want to complicate PaintTile with coordinate calculation. It should get exact pixels to be painted. For
that I need a class which returns coordinates of the cell and returns position and sizes of rect to fill the
map cell. This class must be used in mouseListener cause there i get mouseEvents and also i need cellSize to fill
in paintInstruction sizes. For that purpose MapGrid class was created.

Now let's paint units. Units will be displayed by different shape - circles to differentiate from tiles. Also
they need a new instruction for them. There also arises a problem of painting tile beneath unit, cause tile
will cover a unit, and there's a need for a way to repaint it again. The solution is to store units in hashMap
and on every click on the map check if it currently holds a unit. This solution brings another problem. How do
we get information about the color of the unit. Unit itself should not store information about its color, because
it is not its responsibility, but it may contain information about its in-game type allowing as to create
HashMap<in-game type, Color> to get needed information. The problem comes that i need to change the shape of
painting than i paint over unit. Current solution is to use two instructions in mouseListener. Giving
information about unit to instruction will force a null check when there is no unit in tile.

There is another question how should i paint shapes. First is to use to different instructions or should i put
brush object inside instruction. Brush object will contain shape and color and polyform method to paint it. Second
will force to create multiple brushes and method would be the same with instruction or should i create
HashMap<Shape, inst> in paint instruction but still i will have to deal with unit drama and brings unnecessary
complexity with hashmap and i either will have to use multiple states with hashmap state solution or not.

The problem is what i need to know whether i paint tile or unit before click, I could set Instruction after
clicking button of the unit or tile. After clicking an exact tile or unit i also need to set a color
for instruction. Clicking button sets instruction, clicking exact object sets color. If creating brush object
the shape must be a method, which basically equal the instruction.

I want to set color and shape for PaintTile, but this way I won't be able to polyform it with instruction
interface. First solution is create a new class with aggregated color, shape, which is instruction. I need
to differ form painting and instructions so that i won't paint instruction as form. I need to change color
only after clicking button if i store color at instruction it will change every time fulfilled. However
access to Graphics will only be obtained during paintComponent, meaning i can't set color outside of it.
Solved: every time paintComponent method called there is a new Graphics object, so either way i will have
to set it up again.

How do i set PaintForm. First solution is HashMap. Seconds buttons set instruction in BufferedPanel and
listener than fills needed data, but this way i won't be able to paint tile over unit cause i need to give
instruction of CirclePaint to BufferedPanel from listener, so I need to store instructions in listener.

I can manage instruction color after clicking button in BufferedPaintPanel, avoiding setting a new instruction
every click and MouseListener will only activate it
This won't work cause i return default instruction after every paint, but may be useful if i found solution for setting
default, means i need set instruction every paint action.

(, but there is a issue with painting over unit, it is better
to store name in button listener instead of color, giving us easier opportunity to change colors of
different in-game types of units and also allows to change color in runtime, so we also need a hashMap of
instructions cause storing name of category(unit, terrain) is more versatile allowing to draw different
categories differently. So i need a class that will store hashMap of colors and PaintForms. I can create new
method at BufferedPainPanelV2 which receives units in-game type to repaint it again. Pallet, canvas and painter.
Pallet chooses color and form, canvas only paints and painter determines the logic of the paint.
create a new PaintForm for unit which is fine cause it will be stored in stack, but can i get access to already
existing PaintCircle. However this method will be more problematic with instruction field at BufferedPaintPanelV2)

Should i store current PaintForm at BufferedPaintPanel or should i store at Painter. BufferedPaintPanel should stay simple.
Buffered paint panel becomes too deep to access instruction maybe i should use inheritance instead
cause it is stored in heap.

MapGrid is responsible for converting point of click in the rect for the cell that needs to be filled.
MapData stores information about already placed tiles and units.

Instead of checking type of paintForm for repainting I will use paintLayer int field. And then give array of paintInstructions
to the PaintPanel;

Clicking on button sets shape.

When i chose unit in sideBar I should set brush to circle